    _cache[1] || (_cache[1] = createStaticVNode('<h3 id="_3-hardware-wiring" tabindex="-1">(3) Hardware Wiring <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-hardware-wiring" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(3) Hardware Wiring&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Connect the left wheel 360° block motor to port <strong>S1</strong> of the controller using a 3-pin servo cable.</li><li>Connect the right wheel 360° block motor to port <strong>S2</strong> of the controller using a 3-pin servo cable.</li><li>Connect the WonderMind module to port <strong>A</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="6248" width="10520" src="' + _imports_0 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><h3 id="_4-adding-extensions" tabindex="-1">(4) Adding Extensions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-adding-extensions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(4) Adding Extensions&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Select <strong>WonderMind AI</strong> <strong>large model module</strong> under the <strong>Input Module</strong> category.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><ul><li>Select <strong>360° Motor</strong> under the <strong>Motion module</strong> category.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_2 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><h3 id="_5-core-blocks" tabindex="-1">(5) Core Blocks <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-core-blocks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(5) Core Blocks&quot;">​</a></h3><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">Block</th><th style="text-align:left;">Category</th><th style="text-align:left;">Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="99" width="982" src="' + _imports_101 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="71" width="77" src="' + _imports_99 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Drives the buzzer to play notes at specified pitch and beat duration, runs in background mode without blocking subsequent program execution.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="151" width="345" src="' + _imports_109 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="71" width="77" src="' + _imports_99 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Real-time detection of specified button current level state, returns boolean value indicating whether button is pressed.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="95" width="384" src="' + _imports_6 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="80" src="' + _imports_7 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Initializes the hardware interface of the WonderMind AI large model module.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="85" width="446" src="' + _imports_8 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="80" src="' + _imports_7 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Loads default MCP protocol parameter configuration to complete basic large model interaction channel setup.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="86" width="260" src="' + _imports_9 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="80" src="' + _imports_7 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Activates the MCP conversation channel to enable large model interaction functions.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="177" width="664" src="' + _imports_10 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="80" src="' + _imports_7 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Pushes action completion command to the large model to notify that current robot action execution is finished.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="60" width="227" src="' + _imports_11 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="80" src="' + _imports_7 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Reads reply commands and text data issued by the large model for subsequent program calls.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="359" width="402" src="' + _imports_12 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="80" src="' + _imports_7 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Issues motion control commands to the robot, dropdown menu provides multiple robot actions and device control commands.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="337" width="337" src="' + _imports_13 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="53" width="80" src="' + _imports_7 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Reads corresponding parameter values from large model issued commands, dropdown selection provides various parameters for acquiring action speed, running duration, light color, servo angle, ranging distance, and other data for program logic calls.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="145" width="655" src="' + _imports_14 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="71" width="80" src="' + _imports_15 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Controls the specified port 360° block motor with custom running speed, select forward or reverse rotation direction for continuous operation.</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="91" width="416" src="' + _imports_16 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;"><img loading="lazy" height="71" width="80" src="' + _imports_15 + '"></td><td style="text-align:left;">Cuts output to the specified port 360° block motor, causing the motor to stop rotating immediately.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="_6-program-description-and-upload" tabindex="-1">(6) Program Description and Upload <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-program-description-and-upload" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;(6) Program Description and Upload&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Program Schematic</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="701" width="430" src="' + _imports_17 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><ul><li><p>Complete Program</p><ol><li><p>After the controller powers on, a startup sound plays. When the WonderMind interface displays MCP configuration, press the left button on the controller. The buzzer then plays a ready sound.</p></li><li><p>Continuously cycle to retrieve data sent from WonderMind to the controller, store as &quot;return data,&quot; then check whether &quot;return data&quot; contains voice action commands.</p></li><li><p>Extract action direction, running speed, and duration parameters from &quot;return data.&quot; Set default parameters as fallback. Drive S1 and S2 motors to complete corresponding movement actions. Delay ends and motors stop. After the complete action set finishes, push action completion information.</p></li></ol></li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="1220" width="651" src="' + _imports_18 + '" class="common_img"><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p></p><p><strong>The source files are available for download under <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1cIZD1yG0hj_lme2SCWtL_KWlalJhd6ib?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Program Files</a>.</strong></p></div><ul><li>Program Upload: When uploading the program, <strong>first power on the controller, then power on WonderMind</strong> <strong>switch</strong>.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="360" width="640" src="' + _imports_122 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><ul><li><strong>Device restart:</strong> After successful program upload, the large model module requires <strong>restart</strong> before program verification. <strong>The power-on sequence must strictly follow controller first, then WonderMind. First, in the field ①, then in the field ②. Wait for WonderMind to enter the Waiting for MCP extension tool configuration, press Button A to skip interface, then press the left button on the controller to run the program normally</strong>. If the uploaded program is unchanged, the WonderMind restart step is not required.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="1310" width="3341" src="' + _imports_20 + '" class="common_img"><h2 id="_3-8-5-extended-knowledge" tabindex="-1">3.8.5 Extended Knowledge <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-8-5-extended-knowledge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.8.5 Extended Knowledge&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>(1) Segmented Parsing of Continuous Compound Voice Commands</strong></p><p>Perform text segmentation on long-form voice input to extract multiple action and duration parameter sets. Achieve serial execution of continuous action sequences such as &quot;move forward 3 seconds, turn right 2 seconds, move backward 1 second.&quot; The underlying implementation relies on MCP parameter multi-segment reading logic. String splitting completes queued execution of multiple commands. Extends to campus patrol robots with single voice command issuance of complete patrol routes.</p><p><strong>(2) Abnormal Command Voice Feedback Mechanism</strong></p><p>Add command validity judgment branches. When voice content lacks valid motion parameters or recognition is ambiguous, WonderMind automatically broadcasts prompt voice. Semantic layered recognition capability filters invalid text to improve human-robot interaction fault tolerance. Applicable to elderly smart personal mobility voice vehicles.</p><h2 id="_3-8-6-q-a" tabindex="-1">3.8.6 Q&amp;A <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-8-6-q-a" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.8.6 Q&amp;A&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Question 1: What advantages do triangular tracks have compared to ordinary tracks?</strong></p><p>Answer: Triangular tracks have a larger ground contact area, providing stronger traction and better passability. They offer higher stability in complex terrain such as sandy ground and staircases, making them less prone to tipping over.</p><p><strong>Question 2:</strong> <strong>What chassis movements correspond to motor forward rotation and reverse rotation?</strong></p><p>Answer: Standard definition: both motors rotating forward moves the chassis forward; reverse rotation moves the chassis backward. When both motors rotate in opposite directions, the chassis rotates in place.</p><h2 id="_3-8-7-technical-support-and-product-discussion" tabindex="-1">3.8.7 Technical Support and Product Discussion <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-8-7-technical-support-and-product-discussion" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3.8.7 Technical Support and Product Discussion&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If creative ideas emerge after using the product, sharing in the <a href="https://forum.hiwonder.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hiwonder community</a> is welcome:</p><a href="https://forum.hiwonder.com.cn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img loading="lazy" height="640" width="1136" src="' + _imports_12$1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"></a>', 33))
